{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793382936,"nonce":"+1V8ldUJAkvCBxZG52ySY4zyFKs=","message":"Correction (darmie): dynASB was built on a Mac and has not run on real Linux/KVM, so its Linux GPU lines are design claims, not results. The GPU session class therefore starts with making dynASB (libkrun with virtio-gpu and Venus) work on the NUC, not with wiring it into the runner. NUC probe, read-only: x86_64 Ubuntu, 16 cores, only 6 GB RAM (about 3 free), 66 GB disk free; KVM present; Intel Iris Xe; Mesa 26.0.3 with the Intel (anv) and virtio Vulkan ICDs, lavapipe, and virglrenderer 1.2.0 (newer than the 0.10.4e that broke Venus on the Mac, and Mesa-matched); cargo nightly, gcc and cmake present; libkrun, libkrunfw and dynASB are not installed. Steps: (1) build libkrun and libkrunfw there and boot a trivial guest; (2) enable virtio-gpu Venus and run vulkaninfo in the guest against the host Intel GPU; (3) only then test GL clients over Zink or virgl (Heaps) and ashui's wgpu through Vulkan; (4) encode and stream; (5) the runner backend and entitlement. RAM is the first constraint: a microVM plus a build will contend in 6 GB.","files":null}]}